 ## Background
-FisherRF was introduced by W. Jiang et al. in the paper "FisherRF: Active View Selection and Mapping with Radiance Fields Using Fisher Information" [S1]. It addresses the problem of active "training" view selection for volumetric rendering, a problem that was extensively studied before radiance fields but lacked literature specific to them [S1]. The method builds on the framework of 3D Gaussian Splatting and Plenoxels, leveraging their differentiable nature and parameter structure [S1]. It improves upon previous methods like Goli et al. by computing the Hessian directly rather than relying on hypothetical perturbation fields and multiple backward passes [S1].
+FisherRF was introduced by W. Jiang et al. in the paper "FisherRF: Active View Selection and Mapping with Radiance Fields Using Fisher Information" [S2]. It addresses the problem of active "training" view selection for volumetric rendering, a problem that was extensively studied before radiance fields but lacked literature specific to them [S2]. The method builds on the framework of 3D Gaussian Splatting and Plenoxels, leveraging their differentiable nature and parameter structure [S2]. It improves upon previous methods like Goli et al. by computing the Hessian directly rather than relying on hypothetical perturbation fields and multiple backward passes [S2].
 
